WEBVTT

NOTE
This file was generated by Descript <www.descript.com>

00:00:04.991 --> 00:00:08.081
Hello, hello, hello, and welcome

00:00:08.081 --> 00:00:10.161
to English Learning for Curious

00:00:10.161 --> 00:00:13.281
Minds, by Leonardo English, the show

00:00:13.291 --> 00:00:15.161
where you can listen to fascinating

00:00:15.161 --> 00:00:17.671
stories and learn weird and wonderful

00:00:17.681 --> 00:00:19.701
things about the world at the same

00:00:19.701 --> 00:00:21.501
time as improving your English.

00:00:21.961 --> 00:00:25.561
I'm Alastair Budge, and today we are

00:00:25.561 --> 00:00:28.181
going to be talking about Muhammad Ali.

00:00:28.711 --> 00:00:31.331
It's a story about a boxing champion,

00:00:31.721 --> 00:00:33.961
obviously, but it's also one of

00:00:33.961 --> 00:00:36.811
identity, civil rights, religion,

00:00:37.251 --> 00:00:40.201
courage, and the cost of greatness.

00:00:40.661 --> 00:00:42.311
We have a lot to get through

00:00:42.311 --> 00:00:45.241
today, so let's get right into it.

00:00:47.041 --> 00:00:49.891
If someone told you their name was

00:00:49.941 --> 00:00:53.061
Cassius Marcellus, your first thought

00:00:53.101 --> 00:00:55.181
might be that their parents were

00:00:55.241 --> 00:00:58.341
unusually passionate about Ancient Rome.

00:00:59.071 --> 00:01:02.491
But Cassius Marcellus Clay, the boy

00:01:02.721 --> 00:01:06.195
born in Louisville, Kentucky, in 1942,

00:01:06.551 --> 00:01:08.881
was not given this name because of

00:01:08.881 --> 00:01:11.831
any passion for the Romans; he had

00:01:11.841 --> 00:01:14.221
the name because his father was given

00:01:14.221 --> 00:01:17.041
it before him, after a 19th century

00:01:17.261 --> 00:01:19.781
Kentucky politician of the same name.

00:01:20.581 --> 00:01:23.041
And perhaps that politician’s parents

00:01:23.221 --> 00:01:26.011
really were fans of Ancient Rome, given

00:01:26.191 --> 00:01:29.327
that they named another son Brutus Junius.

00:01:30.021 --> 00:01:32.871
Now, this original Cassius Marcellus

00:01:32.871 --> 00:01:35.481
Clay was an interesting character in

00:01:35.481 --> 00:01:38.251
his own right; he came from a wealthy

00:01:38.251 --> 00:01:41.131
and powerful slave-owning family before

00:01:41.131 --> 00:01:43.551
becoming an anti-slavery activist.

00:01:43.881 --> 00:01:47.591
But today we are talking about the

00:01:47.691 --> 00:01:50.861
other Cassius Marcellus Clay, Cassius

00:01:51.081 --> 00:01:54.521
Marcellus Clay Junior, the man more

00:01:54.521 --> 00:01:57.741
commonly known by the name Muhammad Ali.

00:01:59.031 --> 00:02:00.981
At the start of our story, of

00:02:00.981 --> 00:02:03.231
course, he was still Cassius.

00:02:04.181 --> 00:02:07.001
His mother, Odessa, was a domestic

00:02:07.001 --> 00:02:09.371
worker, serving a white family.

00:02:09.951 --> 00:02:12.411
And his father, Cassius Senior,

00:02:12.861 --> 00:02:14.691
painted signs for a living.

00:02:15.501 --> 00:02:18.591
It was, by all accounts, an ordinary

00:02:18.591 --> 00:02:21.321
childhood: church on Sundays, a

00:02:21.321 --> 00:02:24.191
modest house, and parents who worked

00:02:24.191 --> 00:02:26.641
hard to provide for their children.

00:02:27.751 --> 00:02:30.491
But this was America of the

00:02:30.491 --> 00:02:33.981
1940s and 1950s, an America

00:02:34.241 --> 00:02:36.261
that was still deeply divided.

00:02:36.811 --> 00:02:39.951
Black people could live, work, and move

00:02:40.101 --> 00:02:42.751
with some freedom in certain parts of a

00:02:42.751 --> 00:02:45.501
city; in others, they were not welcome

00:02:45.501 --> 00:02:48.501
at all, and they were constantly reminded

00:02:48.717 --> 00:02:50.981
that they were second-class citizens.

00:02:51.921 --> 00:02:54.591
Now, the story of how Cassius Clay

00:02:54.771 --> 00:02:57.981
found boxing has become part of boxing

00:02:58.011 --> 00:03:00.941
folklore, and it goes like this.

00:03:01.741 --> 00:03:03.411
He was twelve years old.

00:03:03.921 --> 00:03:06.021
He had gone to a fair with some

00:03:06.021 --> 00:03:09.391
friends, and left his bike outside.

00:03:10.261 --> 00:03:11.971
When he returned, he

00:03:11.971 --> 00:03:13.961
found the bike was gone.

00:03:14.561 --> 00:03:16.491
Someone had stolen it.

00:03:17.241 --> 00:03:20.061
He was distraught; it was his pride

00:03:20.101 --> 00:03:22.861
and joy, his parents had saved

00:03:22.891 --> 00:03:25.531
up to buy it for him, and someone

00:03:25.981 --> 00:03:28.081
had snatched it away from him.

00:03:29.471 --> 00:03:32.881
He asked around, and was directed to a

00:03:32.891 --> 00:03:36.441
local police officer called Joe Martin.

00:03:37.531 --> 00:03:39.811
Martin patiently listened as the

00:03:39.811 --> 00:03:42.033
young boy threatened to "whup"

00:03:42.381 --> 00:03:43.901
the thief when he found him.

00:03:45.271 --> 00:03:47.791
At this time, Cassius Clay was a

00:03:47.821 --> 00:03:50.381
scrawny little twelve-year-old, so

00:03:50.671 --> 00:03:53.261
Martin, the police officer, decided

00:03:53.391 --> 00:03:55.291
he needed to give him some advice,

00:03:55.651 --> 00:03:58.821
advice that has become legend: "well,

00:03:59.271 --> 00:04:01.321
you'd better learn how to fight first".

00:04:02.751 --> 00:04:06.451
Now, Cassius Clay never got his bike back,

00:04:06.641 --> 00:04:09.731
and never managed to "whup" the thief.

00:04:10.551 --> 00:04:12.841
But the meeting, and this

00:04:12.871 --> 00:04:15.801
advice, would change his life.

00:04:16.551 --> 00:04:19.791
See, Joe Martin, the police officer,

00:04:20.241 --> 00:04:24.251
also ran a boxing gym, and suggested

00:04:24.271 --> 00:04:26.851
the young boy come and do some training.

00:04:27.741 --> 00:04:30.681
He did, and by all accounts,

00:04:30.861 --> 00:04:32.631
he was instantly hooked.

00:04:33.251 --> 00:04:35.551
He was there before school the

00:04:35.551 --> 00:04:37.351
following morning, and the morning

00:04:37.361 --> 00:04:39.851
after that, and the morning after that.

00:04:41.021 --> 00:04:43.621
Cassius was never the biggest boy in

00:04:43.621 --> 00:04:46.051
the gym, nor was he the strongest.

00:04:46.651 --> 00:04:49.421
What he had was speed and a

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:51.501
dedication that Martin had not

00:04:51.511 --> 00:04:54.021
seen before in someone so young.

00:04:54.901 --> 00:04:57.621
Six days a week, every week,

00:04:57.781 --> 00:04:59.371
he was there before dawn.

00:04:59.811 --> 00:05:00.501
He ran.

00:05:00.511 --> 00:05:01.861
He skipped rope.

00:05:02.031 --> 00:05:03.271
He worked the bags.

00:05:03.321 --> 00:05:04.531
He sparred.

00:05:05.121 --> 00:05:08.281
Most boys his age would come down and hit

00:05:08.321 --> 00:05:11.841
a few bags, but nobody took it anywhere

00:05:11.841 --> 00:05:14.841
near as seriously as young Cassius Clay.

00:05:16.061 --> 00:05:18.021
And there was something else that

00:05:18.031 --> 00:05:21.361
struck Martin: the boy's fearless,

00:05:21.805 --> 00:05:25.111
unshakeable confidence in his own ability.

00:05:26.821 --> 00:05:29.321
Martin started to enter him in

00:05:29.321 --> 00:05:31.821
local competitions, and against

00:05:31.861 --> 00:05:34.241
all expectations, he won.

00:05:35.141 --> 00:05:36.761
He would fight against bigger,

00:05:37.211 --> 00:05:39.831
heavier, and older opponents, who

00:05:40.101 --> 00:05:41.999
on paper should have beaten him.

00:05:42.728 --> 00:05:44.641
But he kept winning.

00:05:45.971 --> 00:05:48.741
By eighteen, he was the best amateur

00:05:48.741 --> 00:05:51.111
boxer in the United States, and

00:05:51.301 --> 00:05:54.201
people around him were urging him to

00:05:54.291 --> 00:05:57.041
turn professional and start earning.

00:05:58.411 --> 00:06:00.161
He had other plans, at least

00:06:00.491 --> 00:06:01.761
in the immediate future.

00:06:02.591 --> 00:06:05.401
The 1960 Olympic Games were around

00:06:05.411 --> 00:06:08.211
the corner, Cassius Clay wanted a

00:06:08.231 --> 00:06:11.451
gold medal, and professional boxers

00:06:11.691 --> 00:06:13.441
weren't allowed at the Olympics.

00:06:14.901 --> 00:06:16.761
So, he stayed amateur,

00:06:17.021 --> 00:06:18.271
at least for a while.

00:06:19.271 --> 00:06:21.771
There was another logistical problem too.

00:06:22.521 --> 00:06:26.041
The Olympics were in Rome, and not

00:06:26.081 --> 00:06:28.831
only had Clay never been on a plane

00:06:28.831 --> 00:06:32.421
before, but he was terrified of flying.

00:06:33.551 --> 00:06:36.231
He agreed to get on the plane on the

00:06:36.231 --> 00:06:39.001
condition that he could wear a parachute.

00:06:39.541 --> 00:06:42.351
He wore it for the entire flight, and

00:06:42.351 --> 00:06:44.921
fortunately, he did not need to use it.

00:06:45.981 --> 00:06:48.751
At the Olympics, he won every fight.

00:06:49.191 --> 00:06:51.641
He won the light heavyweight gold medal.

00:06:52.661 --> 00:06:54.881
Not only this, but the Olympics

00:06:54.951 --> 00:06:56.711
showed a side to the young man

00:06:57.081 --> 00:06:59.321
that most people weren't aware of.

00:06:59.861 --> 00:07:03.281
He was charming, curious, funny,

00:07:03.701 --> 00:07:05.971
and not afraid to speak his mind.

00:07:06.531 --> 00:07:08.281
He wandered through the Olympic

00:07:08.281 --> 00:07:10.921
Village asking questions and making

00:07:10.921 --> 00:07:12.631
friends with competitors from

00:07:12.631 --> 00:07:14.471
countries he had never heard of.

00:07:15.451 --> 00:07:17.691
A Soviet journalist, who was looking

00:07:17.691 --> 00:07:20.441
to score a point about American racial

00:07:20.441 --> 00:07:23.851
politics, asked him how he felt about

00:07:23.851 --> 00:07:26.561
competing for a country where his people

00:07:26.731 --> 00:07:28.861
were treated as second-class citizens.

00:07:30.251 --> 00:07:33.291
Clay looked at him and said: "Tell

00:07:33.291 --> 00:07:35.231
your readers we’ve got qualified

00:07:35.241 --> 00:07:37.231
people working on that problem, and

00:07:37.521 --> 00:07:38.941
I’m not worried about the outcome.

00:07:39.481 --> 00:07:42.091
To me, the USA is still the best

00:07:42.101 --> 00:07:44.381
country in the world, counting yours."

00:07:45.821 --> 00:07:48.021
He came home with the gold medal

00:07:48.051 --> 00:07:50.641
around his neck, and in October

00:07:50.821 --> 00:07:53.361
1960 he turned professional.

00:07:54.381 --> 00:07:57.021
Now, we aren't going to talk too

00:07:57.031 --> 00:07:59.961
much about his boxing history, but

00:08:00.301 --> 00:08:02.111
there are some important things

00:08:02.111 --> 00:08:04.261
to mention when it came to style.

00:08:05.521 --> 00:08:07.851
Back then, there were certain

00:08:07.871 --> 00:08:10.691
expectations about what a heavyweight

00:08:10.711 --> 00:08:12.771
boxer was meant to look like,

00:08:12.791 --> 00:08:14.851
and how they were meant to fight.

00:08:16.151 --> 00:08:18.831
Heavyweight champions were supposed to be

00:08:19.181 --> 00:08:22.031
powerful and physical and overwhelming.

00:08:22.461 --> 00:08:25.561
They stood in, absorbed punishment,

00:08:25.681 --> 00:08:27.941
and delivered it in return.

00:08:28.591 --> 00:08:29.951
That was the tradition.

00:08:30.401 --> 00:08:33.521
Two giant men landing huge

00:08:33.531 --> 00:08:35.411
blows against each other until

00:08:35.421 --> 00:08:37.261
one was declared the winner.

00:08:38.831 --> 00:08:40.981
Clay was different.

00:08:41.431 --> 00:08:42.881
He moved.

00:08:42.881 --> 00:08:43.961
He circled.

00:08:44.171 --> 00:08:46.531
He kept his hands low, which

00:08:46.581 --> 00:08:48.711
every coach told him was wrong,

00:08:48.761 --> 00:08:50.901
because it left his body exposed.

00:08:51.271 --> 00:08:53.521
You should keep your hands up to protect

00:08:53.521 --> 00:08:56.041
yourself and make it easier to punch back.

00:08:57.001 --> 00:08:58.531
No, Clay said.

00:08:58.871 --> 00:09:01.481
His reflexes were fast enough that

00:09:01.481 --> 00:09:04.131
he could move his head and duck out

00:09:04.131 --> 00:09:06.591
of the way before the punch arrived.

00:09:08.301 --> 00:09:12.281
And this strategy, well, it worked.

00:09:13.081 --> 00:09:14.431
If you've watched many of his

00:09:14.431 --> 00:09:16.191
fights, you'll know what I mean.

00:09:16.601 --> 00:09:19.021
He almost dances around the ring with

00:09:19.021 --> 00:09:21.431
his arms by his side, his opponent

00:09:21.671 --> 00:09:24.271
tries to land a knockout blow, only

00:09:24.301 --> 00:09:26.091
for Clay to duck out of the way

00:09:26.281 --> 00:09:28.871
and land a jab before his opponent

00:09:28.881 --> 00:09:30.471
has the time to steady himself.

00:09:32.021 --> 00:09:35.341
Still, many boxing commentators watching

00:09:35.341 --> 00:09:38.529
his early fights were unconvinced.

00:09:39.021 --> 00:09:41.661
This "dancing" style tired him

00:09:41.661 --> 00:09:43.821
out and left his body exposed.

00:09:44.251 --> 00:09:46.121
He would be found out eventually

00:09:46.371 --> 00:09:48.271
by someone fast enough and

00:09:48.271 --> 00:09:50.641
smart enough to take advantage.

00:09:51.941 --> 00:09:53.981
In his early career, at

00:09:53.991 --> 00:09:56.751
least, that person never came.

00:09:58.341 --> 00:10:00.881
And around the same time, he

00:10:00.901 --> 00:10:03.551
added another string to his bow.

00:10:04.871 --> 00:10:08.661
When he was nineteen, he met a man named

00:10:08.671 --> 00:10:11.171
George Wagner, who was better known

00:10:11.231 --> 00:10:14.371
by his stage name, Gorgeous George.

00:10:15.241 --> 00:10:18.281
Now, Gorgeous George was a professional

00:10:18.281 --> 00:10:20.641
wrestler, and while he was not the

00:10:20.641 --> 00:10:23.931
most technically gifted athlete, he had

00:10:23.931 --> 00:10:26.901
become incredibly famous for creating

00:10:27.101 --> 00:10:30.121
an outrageous, over-the-top persona.

00:10:30.821 --> 00:10:33.401
He dyed his hair peroxide blonde,

00:10:33.751 --> 00:10:36.211
he would be carried to the ring on a

00:10:36.211 --> 00:10:38.691
velvet cushion, he would spray perfume

00:10:38.691 --> 00:10:41.401
into the air, and declare himself "the

00:10:41.401 --> 00:10:43.381
greatest wrestler who ever lived."

00:10:44.891 --> 00:10:47.881
It was this complete self-confidence and

00:10:48.131 --> 00:10:50.501
flamboyance that brought in the crowds,

00:10:50.701 --> 00:10:54.071
and he spelled this out to Cassius Clay.

00:10:55.551 --> 00:10:57.211
"A lot of people will pay to

00:10:57.211 --> 00:10:58.771
see someone shut your mouth,"

00:10:59.221 --> 00:11:00.691
George reportedly told him.

00:11:01.091 --> 00:11:04.181
"So give them a reason to want to."

00:11:05.101 --> 00:11:07.831
In other words, be so arrogant

00:11:07.881 --> 00:11:10.391
and confident that people come

00:11:10.671 --> 00:11:12.541
in the hope of watching you lose.

00:11:13.611 --> 00:11:16.461
Now, Clay already had the talent to

00:11:16.461 --> 00:11:19.791
compete, but with Gorgeous George, he had

00:11:19.791 --> 00:11:22.661
a template for how to attract attention.

00:11:23.741 --> 00:11:25.711
He began to talk about himself

00:11:25.771 --> 00:11:28.311
constantly, telling people that he was

00:11:28.311 --> 00:11:31.701
the greatest boxer ever to live, years

00:11:31.931 --> 00:11:33.741
before he became a world champion.

00:11:34.971 --> 00:11:38.121
And in February of 1964, the

00:11:38.171 --> 00:11:40.311
twenty-two-year-old Clay had the

00:11:40.341 --> 00:11:42.801
biggest fight of his career so far.

00:11:43.671 --> 00:11:46.031
His opponent was Sonny Liston,

00:11:46.491 --> 00:11:47.941
the heavyweight champion.

00:11:49.351 --> 00:11:50.911
Liston was one of the

00:11:50.911 --> 00:11:52.921
most feared men in boxing.

00:11:53.501 --> 00:11:56.131
He was a former criminal with enormous

00:11:56.131 --> 00:11:59.551
fists and a reputation for ending fights

00:11:59.611 --> 00:12:02.441
very quickly and very unpleasantly.

00:12:03.791 --> 00:12:06.821
The bookmakers made Liston an overwhelming

00:12:06.851 --> 00:12:10.211
favourite, and most commentators expected

00:12:10.211 --> 00:12:12.861
the fight to be over within a few rounds.

00:12:13.971 --> 00:12:16.961
Before the fight, Clay was his usual

00:12:17.121 --> 00:12:19.361
antagonistic self, proclaiming:

00:12:19.801 --> 00:12:21.452
"Clay comes out to meet Liston,

00:12:21.478 --> 00:12:23.101
and Liston starts to retreat.

00:12:23.411 --> 00:12:25.201
If Liston goes back any further,

00:12:25.471 --> 00:12:27.561
he'll end up in a ringside seat."

00:12:29.321 --> 00:12:31.621
The fight ended as Clay had

00:12:31.651 --> 00:12:34.871
predicted, and Cassius Clay became

00:12:34.871 --> 00:12:37.391
the heavyweight champion of the world.

00:12:38.191 --> 00:12:40.431
He ran around the ring shouting:

00:12:40.781 --> 00:12:42.481
"I shook up the world. I am the

00:12:42.481 --> 00:12:44.091
greatest thing that ever lived."

00:12:45.841 --> 00:12:48.901
But soon after the fight, the

00:12:48.901 --> 00:12:51.701
attention shifted away from

00:12:51.701 --> 00:12:53.221
his performance in the ring.

00:12:54.331 --> 00:12:57.231
Clay publicly confirmed what many

00:12:57.231 --> 00:13:00.571
people suspected: he had joined an

00:13:00.581 --> 00:13:03.611
organisation called the Nation of Islam.

00:13:04.671 --> 00:13:08.421
As part of this, he rejected the name

00:13:08.501 --> 00:13:12.031
Cassius Clay, briefly became Cassius X,

00:13:12.411 --> 00:13:14.971
and was then given the name the world

00:13:14.991 --> 00:13:17.871
would come to know him by: Muhammad Ali.

00:13:18.885 --> 00:13:22.005
Now, the Nation of Islam was not a

00:13:22.005 --> 00:13:24.285
mainstream religious organisation.

00:13:25.025 --> 00:13:27.445
It taught that Black Americans

00:13:27.635 --> 00:13:29.485
were the original people of the

00:13:29.485 --> 00:13:31.955
earth and had been subjected to

00:13:31.975 --> 00:13:34.655
centuries of deliberate oppression.

00:13:35.505 --> 00:13:37.865
It preached Black pride, Black

00:13:38.045 --> 00:13:40.885
separatism, and economic self-sufficiency.

00:13:41.605 --> 00:13:43.715
It demanded discipline from its

00:13:43.715 --> 00:13:47.085
members: no alcohol, no tobacco, no

00:13:47.085 --> 00:13:49.485
dependence on white institutions.

00:13:50.145 --> 00:13:53.125
Its leader, at that time at least, was

00:13:53.125 --> 00:13:56.215
a man named Elijah Muhammad, but its

00:13:56.215 --> 00:13:59.085
most prominent public voice was Malcolm

00:13:59.085 --> 00:14:02.465
X. In fact, we have a full episode on

00:14:02.465 --> 00:14:06.155
Malcolm X, episode 294, so that's one to

00:14:06.155 --> 00:14:07.915
listen to if you haven't done so already.

00:14:08.615 --> 00:14:12.015
But back to Cassius Clay, or to

00:14:12.015 --> 00:14:14.915
give him his new name, Muhammad Ali.

00:14:16.185 --> 00:14:19.225
This announcement was a powerful message.

00:14:19.695 --> 00:14:22.235
This was the new heavyweight boxing

00:14:22.235 --> 00:14:25.065
champion of the world publicly coming

00:14:25.065 --> 00:14:27.005
out in support of a group that was

00:14:27.005 --> 00:14:29.145
considered radical by the authorities,

00:14:29.515 --> 00:14:31.325
and treated with some suspicion

00:14:31.595 --> 00:14:33.515
by most of mainstream America.

00:14:34.905 --> 00:14:37.664
And to clarify, this wasn't him

00:14:37.684 --> 00:14:40.165
simply changing faith, switching from

00:14:40.415 --> 00:14:43.005
Baptist Christianity to Islam; it

00:14:43.005 --> 00:14:45.385
wasn't a private religious choice.

00:14:45.675 --> 00:14:48.725
It was a rejection of his past, and

00:14:48.925 --> 00:14:51.555
a public act of self-definition.

00:14:52.265 --> 00:14:55.075
He rejected “Cassius Clay” as

00:14:55.075 --> 00:14:57.295
what he called his slave name,

00:14:57.685 --> 00:14:59.905
a name that connected him to

00:14:59.905 --> 00:15:02.143
a history he had not chosen.

00:15:03.304 --> 00:15:06.484
The message was clear: I am not the

00:15:06.504 --> 00:15:08.805
compliant champion you expected.

00:15:10.315 --> 00:15:12.465
Still, many journalists and

00:15:12.485 --> 00:15:14.805
commentators refused to use

00:15:14.805 --> 00:15:17.365
this name, instead insisting on

00:15:17.365 --> 00:15:19.635
referring to him as Cassius Clay.

00:15:20.905 --> 00:15:22.505
Some opponents did too.

00:15:23.305 --> 00:15:25.945
Ernie Terrell, a heavyweight champion

00:15:26.105 --> 00:15:29.725
who fought Ali in February 1967, refused

00:15:29.765 --> 00:15:31.905
to call him by his name throughout

00:15:31.905 --> 00:15:33.865
the entire build-up to the fight.

00:15:35.345 --> 00:15:37.665
Ali made it very clear

00:15:37.835 --> 00:15:39.315
that this was unacceptable.

00:15:40.585 --> 00:15:41.625
When the fight came,

00:15:41.915 --> 00:15:43.455
Ali won it comfortably.

00:15:43.835 --> 00:15:46.495
But he did not try to end it quickly.

00:15:46.895 --> 00:15:49.973
He punished Terrell round after round,

00:15:50.235 --> 00:15:53.175
and between punches he leaned in close

00:15:53.315 --> 00:15:56.045
and asked the same question, again and

00:15:56.045 --> 00:15:58.915
again: "What's my name? What's my name?"

00:16:00.372 --> 00:16:02.232
Eventually, people stopped

00:16:02.272 --> 00:16:03.842
calling him Cassius Clay.

00:16:05.272 --> 00:16:08.802
Now, from the mid-1960s, Ali was the

00:16:08.802 --> 00:16:11.592
undisputed world heavyweight champion.

00:16:12.052 --> 00:16:15.792
But he was also a young man in

00:16:15.802 --> 00:16:18.982
an America at war with Vietnam.

00:16:20.022 --> 00:16:22.402
The military draft was reaching young

00:16:22.402 --> 00:16:26.992
men across the country, and in 1967, Ali

00:16:27.032 --> 00:16:29.972
was ordered to report for the US army.

00:16:31.282 --> 00:16:33.972
He refused to go, claiming

00:16:34.042 --> 00:16:36.382
conscientious objector status on

00:16:36.382 --> 00:16:38.342
the grounds of his Muslim faith.

00:16:39.832 --> 00:16:43.692
Ali's position was specific: his faith

00:16:44.232 --> 00:16:47.272
held that he could not kill people with

00:16:47.272 --> 00:16:50.932
whom he had no quarrel, and he had no

00:16:50.972 --> 00:16:53.062
quarrel with the Vietnamese people.

00:16:54.202 --> 00:16:57.272
Or, as he famously put it: "I ain't

00:16:57.272 --> 00:16:59.382
got no quarrel with them Viet Cong.

00:16:59.872 --> 00:17:02.072
No Viet Cong ever called me nigger."

00:17:03.862 --> 00:17:06.292
The draft board rejected the claim,

00:17:06.672 --> 00:17:09.062
deciding that his objection was

00:17:09.182 --> 00:17:11.612
political rather than religious.

00:17:12.782 --> 00:17:15.042
And this rejection had

00:17:15.122 --> 00:17:17.072
immediate ramifications.

00:17:17.862 --> 00:17:20.652
He was stripped of his heavyweight title

00:17:20.902 --> 00:17:24.042
and his boxing licence was suspended.

00:17:24.762 --> 00:17:27.562
He was convicted of draft evasion

00:17:27.932 --> 00:17:30.202
and sentenced to five years in

00:17:30.202 --> 00:17:32.722
prison, but he was released on

00:17:32.722 --> 00:17:34.982
bail while the case was appealed.

00:17:35.812 --> 00:17:38.262
He was twenty-five years old, at the

00:17:38.262 --> 00:17:41.972
peak of his physical powers, and not only

00:17:42.302 --> 00:17:45.292
could he not box, but he had this lengthy

00:17:45.292 --> 00:17:47.872
prison sentence hanging over his head.

00:17:49.202 --> 00:17:51.192
He did not go to prison in the end.

00:17:51.862 --> 00:17:55.682
In 1971, the Supreme Court overturned

00:17:55.702 --> 00:17:58.662
his conviction, unanimously, ruling that

00:17:58.662 --> 00:18:01.172
the draft board had not given adequate

00:18:01.182 --> 00:18:04.222
reasons for rejecting his religious claim.

00:18:05.472 --> 00:18:07.422
But the three and a half

00:18:07.422 --> 00:18:09.832
years he had lost were gone.

00:18:11.002 --> 00:18:12.722
Ali did not disappear, though.

00:18:13.502 --> 00:18:15.472
Barred from boxing, he spent

00:18:15.482 --> 00:18:17.032
those years travelling across

00:18:17.032 --> 00:18:19.332
America, speaking at universities

00:18:19.632 --> 00:18:21.392
and to anyone who would listen.

00:18:22.772 --> 00:18:25.352
Many of the students he spoke to were

00:18:25.382 --> 00:18:27.652
themselves wrestling with the draft,

00:18:28.082 --> 00:18:30.672
the Vietnam War, and the growing

00:18:30.672 --> 00:18:33.102
tensions inside American society.

00:18:34.302 --> 00:18:36.982
The underlying argument in all

00:18:37.002 --> 00:18:39.642
of these lectures was serious

00:18:39.752 --> 00:18:41.442
and entirely consistent.

00:18:42.192 --> 00:18:44.572
The United States was asking Black

00:18:44.602 --> 00:18:47.722
Americans to fight overseas for

00:18:47.722 --> 00:18:50.092
freedoms they did not have at home.

00:18:50.822 --> 00:18:52.672
The war was unjust.

00:18:53.252 --> 00:18:54.052
He would not go.

00:18:55.012 --> 00:18:56.912
As the years passed, public

00:18:56.912 --> 00:18:59.102
opinion about Vietnam shifted.

00:18:59.782 --> 00:19:03.572
By 1971, the war was deeply unpopular.

00:19:04.362 --> 00:19:07.142
Ali, who had been denounced as a traitor

00:19:07.222 --> 00:19:10.722
in 1967, was increasingly seen as

00:19:10.732 --> 00:19:13.322
someone who had been right all along.

00:19:14.362 --> 00:19:16.372
The cost of that decision had been

00:19:16.422 --> 00:19:19.252
paid in full: three and a half

00:19:19.252 --> 00:19:22.012
years of his prime, the loss of his

00:19:22.012 --> 00:19:24.472
title, and a criminal conviction.

00:19:26.282 --> 00:19:29.492
Ali was allowed to return to the ring in

00:19:29.502 --> 00:19:33.612
1970, but he returned a different man.

00:19:34.542 --> 00:19:36.752
He was older and heavier.

00:19:37.272 --> 00:19:38.482
His legs were slower.

00:19:38.982 --> 00:19:41.352
He could not move the way he once had,

00:19:41.742 --> 00:19:44.882
and he absorbed punches that the earlier

00:19:44.882 --> 00:19:47.102
version of himself would have slipped.

00:19:48.722 --> 00:19:52.612
He fought Joe Frazier three times, and the

00:19:52.622 --> 00:19:54.982
fights are among the most celebrated in

00:19:54.982 --> 00:19:58.222
boxing history, and the most complicated.

00:19:59.222 --> 00:20:02.192
Both men were Black, both came

00:20:02.192 --> 00:20:03.822
from working-class backgrounds,

00:20:04.102 --> 00:20:06.772
and both were remarkable fighters.

00:20:07.832 --> 00:20:11.082
But the politics and the press turned

00:20:11.092 --> 00:20:13.592
them into symbols of something larger:

00:20:14.482 --> 00:20:17.612
Ali the activist, the Muslim, the

00:20:17.612 --> 00:20:20.072
man who had stood up to authority;

00:20:20.932 --> 00:20:23.812
Frazier cast, somewhat unfairly,

00:20:24.132 --> 00:20:26.042
as the establishment choice.

00:20:27.402 --> 00:20:29.612
Ali lost the first fight,

00:20:29.792 --> 00:20:32.622
in March 1971, on points.

00:20:33.052 --> 00:20:35.432
It was his first professional defeat.

00:20:36.342 --> 00:20:39.192
Then came the Rumble in the Jungle.

00:20:39.582 --> 00:20:43.552
October 1974, Kinshasa, Zaire, the

00:20:43.732 --> 00:20:46.122
modern-day Democratic Republic of Congo.

00:20:47.202 --> 00:20:49.922
His opponent was George Foreman:

00:20:50.352 --> 00:20:53.502
enormous and devastating, regarded at

00:20:53.512 --> 00:20:56.072
that point as one of the most dangerous

00:20:56.152 --> 00:20:58.492
punchers in the history of the sport.

00:20:59.522 --> 00:21:02.802
Foreman had destroyed Frazier, who,

00:21:03.092 --> 00:21:05.052
remember, was the man who had given

00:21:05.092 --> 00:21:07.712
Ali his first professional defeat.

00:21:09.192 --> 00:21:12.022
The fight looked close to impossible.

00:21:13.282 --> 00:21:15.522
The strategy Ali's team had agreed

00:21:15.522 --> 00:21:17.842
on was the one he had always

00:21:17.842 --> 00:21:21.482
used: movement, speed, jabs.

00:21:22.782 --> 00:21:25.442
In the early rounds, he tried it.

00:21:25.642 --> 00:21:29.072
But Foreman was too young, too powerful.

00:21:29.582 --> 00:21:32.022
Those jabs barely troubled him.

00:21:33.372 --> 00:21:36.442
So Ali did something nobody

00:21:36.442 --> 00:21:38.152
in his corner was expecting.

00:21:38.812 --> 00:21:40.642
He went to the ropes.

00:21:42.162 --> 00:21:44.222
His trainer shouted at him

00:21:44.232 --> 00:21:46.132
between rounds to get off them.

00:21:46.962 --> 00:21:48.122
Ali ignored him.

00:21:49.402 --> 00:21:51.322
He had worked something out that he

00:21:51.322 --> 00:21:54.042
hadn't shared with anyone: if he leaned

00:21:54.052 --> 00:21:56.932
back and let Foreman throw punches,

00:21:57.352 --> 00:22:00.012
Foreman would eventually grow tired.

00:22:00.642 --> 00:22:04.492
All Ali had to do was cover up and wait.

00:22:05.642 --> 00:22:08.032
Round after round, Foreman

00:22:08.142 --> 00:22:09.852
threw enormous blows.

00:22:10.672 --> 00:22:13.262
Ali absorbed them and waited.

00:22:14.672 --> 00:22:16.422
By the eighth round,

00:22:16.837 --> 00:22:18.372
Foreman's arms were heavy.

00:22:19.152 --> 00:22:20.892
Ali came off the ropes

00:22:21.152 --> 00:22:22.512
and knocked him out.

00:22:23.752 --> 00:22:26.192
It was perhaps the greatest single

00:22:26.272 --> 00:22:29.082
tactical performance in boxing history.

00:22:29.652 --> 00:22:32.282
By a man who was thirty-two years old,

00:22:32.602 --> 00:22:34.962
who had spent three and a half years

00:22:35.342 --> 00:22:38.422
outside his sport, and whose best

00:22:38.422 --> 00:22:41.102
days looked like they were behind him.

00:22:42.882 --> 00:22:44.902
The third Frazier fight, the

00:22:45.333 --> 00:22:47.232
Thrilla in Manila in October

00:22:47.232 --> 00:22:50.222
1975, was something else entirely.

00:22:51.382 --> 00:22:54.182
Both men were so damaged by the

00:22:54.202 --> 00:22:56.332
fourteenth round that Frazier's

00:22:56.332 --> 00:22:58.682
trainer stopped the fight rather

00:22:58.682 --> 00:23:00.442
than send him out for the fifteenth.

00:23:01.522 --> 00:23:03.872
Ali later said it was the closest

00:23:03.872 --> 00:23:06.452
thing to death he had ever experienced.

00:23:08.062 --> 00:23:10.942
Now, Muhammad Ali fought on

00:23:11.142 --> 00:23:12.332
longer than he should have.

00:23:12.842 --> 00:23:14.312
He lost the title, won

00:23:14.312 --> 00:23:16.172
it back, lost it again.

00:23:17.192 --> 00:23:19.812
By his final years in the ring, the

00:23:19.822 --> 00:23:22.742
fights were increasingly hard to watch.

00:23:23.902 --> 00:23:27.272
There was one fight in 1980, when Ali

00:23:27.272 --> 00:23:30.532
was 38, that was particularly painful.

00:23:31.502 --> 00:23:33.392
It was against a man named

00:23:33.422 --> 00:23:35.452
Larry Holmes, who had grown up

00:23:35.482 --> 00:23:37.992
in complete admiration of Ali.

00:23:38.852 --> 00:23:40.892
Although it went to the tenth

00:23:40.892 --> 00:23:43.332
round, it was so one-sided

00:23:43.782 --> 00:23:45.292
it was almost embarrassing.

00:23:46.022 --> 00:23:48.852
Holmes reportedly wept in his dressing

00:23:48.852 --> 00:23:51.082
room afterwards, saying he felt

00:23:51.112 --> 00:23:52.702
like he had been hitting a ghost.

00:23:54.052 --> 00:23:57.432
Ali retired in 1981 with

00:23:57.432 --> 00:24:00.532
fifty-six wins and five losses.

00:24:02.042 --> 00:24:05.792
And in 1984, he was diagnosed

00:24:05.822 --> 00:24:08.032
with Parkinson's syndrome, the

00:24:08.211 --> 00:24:10.072
neurological condition that

00:24:10.072 --> 00:24:12.112
affects movement and speech.

00:24:13.182 --> 00:24:15.682
He never publicly confirmed that it

00:24:15.682 --> 00:24:17.482
had been caused by the blows he had

00:24:17.482 --> 00:24:19.582
taken over thirty years of boxing.

00:24:19.982 --> 00:24:22.752
But many doctors believed it had.

00:24:23.412 --> 00:24:25.842
He had taken far more damage than was

00:24:25.852 --> 00:24:27.922
good for him, more than his trainers

00:24:27.942 --> 00:24:29.792
and managers should have allowed.

00:24:31.072 --> 00:24:32.642
You might have seen some interviews

00:24:32.642 --> 00:24:34.612
with him in his later years.

00:24:35.112 --> 00:24:37.842
His voice, that quick and

00:24:37.892 --> 00:24:41.382
irreverent voice, became quieter

00:24:41.382 --> 00:24:42.682
and harder to understand.

00:24:43.322 --> 00:24:45.522
His hands started to tremble.

00:24:47.052 --> 00:24:48.952
At the opening ceremony of the

00:24:48.952 --> 00:24:52.402
1996 Atlanta Olympics, Ali was

00:24:52.422 --> 00:24:54.012
chosen to light the cauldron.

00:24:55.152 --> 00:24:57.222
He walked out with the torch, his

00:24:57.232 --> 00:25:00.622
hand trembling visibly, and there was

00:25:00.632 --> 00:25:03.332
almost a collective intake of breath

00:25:03.672 --> 00:25:06.172
as this great champion stood there,

00:25:06.602 --> 00:25:09.052
a shadow of the man he once was.

00:25:10.662 --> 00:25:14.692
He died on 3 June 2016, at

00:25:14.692 --> 00:25:16.322
the age of seventy-four.

00:25:17.482 --> 00:25:18.932
He had refused to fight

00:25:18.942 --> 00:25:20.892
in a war at twenty-five.

00:25:21.102 --> 00:25:22.152
He had been stripped of his

00:25:22.152 --> 00:25:24.152
title and his livelihood.

00:25:24.792 --> 00:25:27.212
He had come back and produced one of the

00:25:27.222 --> 00:25:30.462
greatest second acts in sporting history.

00:25:30.872 --> 00:25:32.752
He had stood before the legal

00:25:32.752 --> 00:25:34.562
system of the most powerful

00:25:34.562 --> 00:25:37.652
country on earth and said no.

00:25:38.382 --> 00:25:39.912
He had chosen his own name,

00:25:40.262 --> 00:25:42.312
and made the world use it.

00:25:43.232 --> 00:25:46.102
He referred to himself as "The Greatest".

00:25:46.532 --> 00:25:48.712
There are only a few people who could

00:25:48.722 --> 00:25:50.982
say that with a straight face and have

00:25:50.992 --> 00:25:52.572
much of the world agree with them.

00:25:53.482 --> 00:25:55.952
Muhammad Ali was certainly one of them.

00:25:58.312 --> 00:26:00.302
OK, then, that is it for

00:26:00.302 --> 00:26:02.652
today's episode on Muhammad Ali.

00:26:03.052 --> 00:26:04.422
I hope it's been an interesting one

00:26:04.672 --> 00:26:06.532
and that you've learnt something new.

00:26:07.182 --> 00:26:09.052
You've been listening to English Learning

00:26:09.052 --> 00:26:11.572
for Curious Minds by Leonardo English.

00:26:11.842 --> 00:26:14.752
I'm Alastair Budge, you stay safe, and

00:26:14.762 --> 00:26:17.412
I'll catch you in the next episode.

